    "result": {
        "response": "The hepatic-related exclusion criterion (Criterion 11) is detailed as follows:\n\n**Exclusion Criterion 11 \u2013 Known clinically relevant chronic liver disease** (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p.11 & p.35):\n\nThis criterion covers two scenarios:\n\n**A) In the absence of a diagnosis of primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)**, a subject is excluded if they have impaired hepatic function defined by ANY of the following:\n- Serum transaminases **>2.5 \u00d7 ULN** (upper limit of normal)\n- Alkaline phosphatase **>2.5 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests judged by the investigator to be **clinically significant**\n\n**B) With a diagnosis of primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)**, a subject is excluded if they have ANY of the following:\n- Serum transaminases **>3 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Alkaline phosphatase **>3 \u00d7 ULN**\n- Abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests with total bilirubin **>1.5 \u00d7 ULN** judged by the investigator to be **clinically significant**\n\nNote that the PSC threshold is slightly more permissive (3\u00d7 vs 2.5\u00d7 ULN for transaminases and ALP), reflecting that PSC patients may have baseline liver abnormalities.\n\nAdditionally, **Criterion 16** also touches on hepatic infections: known concurrent acute or chronic **viral hepatitis B or C infection** is also grounds for exclusion (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p.11 & p.35).",
